A unit value is a magnitude per base, never its own spelling.

`unit measurement { px, em, rem }` declares three independent bases, so
`1px + 2em` keeps both magnitudes and every target represents it as a record
with a field per base.
`unit duration { ms, s = 1000ms, m = 60s, h = 60m }` declares one, so a
duration is a plain number of ms: JS gets the number setInterval already
wants, without any per-language table saying so.

Terms that share a base fold to a single literal before codegen
(combinedUnitLiteral), which is what keeps the common case cheap: `3px + 4px`
is 7px and `1s + 500ms` is 1500ms in every backend, from one place. Only
genuinely mixed bases reach a backend as arithmetic over the record.

Before this, a unit value WAS its source spelling, so `+` concatenated:
`3px + 4px` evaluated to the string "3pxpx4pxpx" in JS, `100ms + 1s` to
"100ms1s", and there was no numeric value at runtime to recover. Kotlin was
worse -- it emitted `(3px + 4px)`, which is not a Kotlin expression, so the
file did not compile. A golden cannot state that a computed value is right, so
the arithmetic is asserted by executing the generated code; this fixture
pins the shape that carries it.

Every one of the five is read. A var nothing reads is shaken, and the two that
carry this archive's explanations -- the reduction and the mixed record -- are
exactly the two a program would have no other reason to keep.

The suffix does not vanish where a host needs it: CSS still wants `7px`.

deny out/none/html/index.html `"[0-9._]*[1-9][0-9._]*(px|em|rem|ms|s)` -- a unit value is a number, never a quoted spelling: `3px + 4px` used to evaluate to "3pxpx4pxpx". A *zero* magnitude is exempt because the display rule spells one: `"0px"` is what a value carrying nothing renders as, and it is a constant rather than a value that was spelled
deny out/kotlin/android/app/src/main/java/test/sngl/app/MainScreen.kt `[^"][0-9](px|rem|em|ms)\b` -- Kotlin emitted the SNGL spelling verbatim (`(3px + 4px)`), which is not a Kotlin expression. Exempt after a quote for the same reason as above -- `"0px"` is the display rule's zero, not a magnitude carrying a suffix

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import "sngl:time"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

component main node {
    // Shares a base: folds to one magnitude in the shared base.
    var samebase measurement = 3px + 4px
    // Reduces through `s = 1000ms`: 1500ms, in ms rather than in s.
    var reduced time.duration = 1s + 500ms
    // Independent bases: no single number, so the record survives.
    var mixed measurement = 1px + 2em

    // One base, so a plain number -- of ms, which is duration's own base.
    var short time.duration = 100ms
    var long time.duration = 1s
    var total time.duration = short + long

    text(value=string(samebase))
    text(value=string(reduced))
    text(value=string(mixed))
    text(value=string(total))
}

window {
    main
}
